<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  S8-33 / AD-014. Apache FOP's configuration for the printed update form.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATE HERE, AND WHY:

  * The two IBM Plex Sans Arabic faces are registered EXPLICITLY, by URL, rather than left to
    FOP's auto-detection. Auto-detection scans the host's own font directories, which means the
    output would depend on what happens to be installed on the machine doing the rendering - a
    developer laptop, a CI container and ECS Fargate would each produce a different document. The
    embedded faces are the ones this repository ships, and nothing else is registered.

  * `embed-url` points at a CLASSPATH resource. The backend is a Spring Boot fat jar; a file path
    would resolve on a developer machine and fail in the deployed image, which is the worst kind of
    difference because it only appears after deployment.

  * .ttf, NEVER .otf. FOP extracts CFF data from an OpenType/CFF font and re-embeds it as Type1C,
    losing exactly the GSUB layout features that join Arabic script. Plain TrueType keeps them.
    The generator that produces these files (mobile/tool/fetch_and_subset_fonts.py) writes .ttf and
    its docstring records why.

  * NO `<complex-scripts disabled="true"/>` element, and there must never be one. Complex-script
    support is ON by default in FOP, and turning it off is how a connected script silently renders
    as disconnected isolated letters with no error of any kind.

  * `strict-configuration` and `strict-validation` are ON. A mistyped font triplet should fail the
    render loudly rather than fall back to a default face and produce a document nobody asked for.

  * `source-resolution` / `target-resolution` are FOP's defaults, stated rather than omitted
    because the portraits and the signature are raster images placed at a physical size.
-->
<fop version="1.0">

  <strict-configuration>true</strict-configuration>
  <strict-validation>true</strict-validation>

  <source-resolution>72</source-resolution>
  <target-resolution>72</target-resolution>

  <!--
    NO <hyphenation-base>. It sets the base URI for user-supplied hyphenation pattern files, and
    an earlier draft of this file carried one pointing at "." under a comment about Arabic that
    did not describe it. Nothing here sets hyphenate="true", so it was dead - and a relative base
    would have contradicted this file's own principle above that the output must not depend on the
    machine doing the rendering. If the form ever needs hyphenation, add it then, with a
    classpath-scoped value.
  -->

  <renderers>
    <renderer mime="application/pdf">

      <!--
        PDF/A is NOT requested. Product-owner ruling, 2026-09-14 (S8-33): plain PDF now, and the
        question of archival conformance goes to the bank separately. Turning it on later changes
        font-embedding rules - full embedding, no subsetting - so it is a deliberate decision and
        this line is where it would be taken.
      -->

      <fonts>
        <font kerning="yes"
              embed-url="classpath:fonts/IBMPlexSansArabic-Regular.ttf"
              embedding-mode="subset">
          <font-triplet name="IBM Plex Sans Arabic" style="normal" weight="normal"/>
        </font>
        <!--
          THE 700 TRIPLET IS NOT OPTIONAL, and leaving it out is a silent failure rather than a
          loud one. Found live at the S8-33 acceptance render: XSL-FO's `font-weight="bold"`
          resolves to the NUMERIC weight 700, so a face registered only at 600 is not a match.
          FOP then substitutes "any,normal,700", which on this machine resolved to the built-in
          Times-Bold, and Times has no Arabic at all - every bold Arabic word on the page became
          a row of missing-glyph events. The document still rendered and still had three pages.

          IBM Plex Sans Arabic ships Regular and SemiBold and no 700 weight, so SemiBold answers
          for both 600 and 700 deliberately: it is the heaviest face the brand bundles, and
          AD-012's typography ruling uses SemiBold for exactly the emphasis the form asks for.
        -->
        <font kerning="yes"
              embed-url="classpath:fonts/IBMPlexSansArabic-SemiBold.ttf"
              embedding-mode="subset">
          <font-triplet name="IBM Plex Sans Arabic" style="normal" weight="700"/>
          <font-triplet name="IBM Plex Sans Arabic" style="normal" weight="600"/>
        </font>
        <!--
          Amiri, for ONE span: the app name «بياناتي» in the printed form's header, set the way the
          mobile splash screen sets it. AD-022 (i), 2026-09-18. Ticket 08 decision 2 still governs
          the rest of the form, which is IBM Plex Sans Arabic throughout.

          REGISTERED AT normal/normal ONLY, and that is not an oversight to be helpfully corrected.
          The generator bundles Amiri in Regular alone (a bold Naskh at display size reads as heavy
          rather than emphatic), so there is no 700 face to point a triplet at. The consequence is
          the failure mode the block above describes, in the one place it can still happen: an
          `fo:` span that INHERITS font-weight="bold" finds no Amiri match, FOP substitutes
          "any,normal,700", and on a machine with Times that renders the Arabic as nothing at all.
          The form pins that span to font-weight="normal" explicitly rather than relying on what it
          happens to inherit.
        -->
        <font kerning="yes"
              embed-url="classpath:fonts/Amiri-Regular.ttf"
              embedding-mode="subset">
          <font-triplet name="Amiri" style="normal" weight="normal"/>
        </font>
      </fonts>

    </renderer>
  </renderers>

</fop>
